      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129504/assets/d0785d11a903993b04ee55c7fc23f8f219280a45b66df759cbadc1d42fc8c418.png)\n\nWhat are the $xy$-coordinates of the point shown in the axes above?",
      "worked_solution": "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$ and $y$ axes.\n\nThe point is $2$ units to the right of the origin, in the positive direction of the $x$-axis. So, the point has an $x$-coordinate of $2.$\n\nAdditionally, the point is $4$ units above the origin, in the positive direction of the $y$-axis. So, the point has a $y$-coordinate of $4.$\n\nThe point has an $x$-coordinate of $2$ and a $y$-coordinate of $4,$ so its coordinates are $(2,4).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
